            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "tkdRi",
              "name": "Notes",
              "width": 490,
              "height": 2353,
              "layout": "vertical",
              "gap": 40,
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "id": "n47NeU",
                  "name": "Note / The Model on Web",
                  "width": 490,
                  "height": 0,
                  "content": "SEARCH & ADD ON WEB — HOW IT WORKS\n\nThree searches now exist, and they are still deliberately NOT siblings. They differ on scope, on what a result IS, and on whether the library grows, shrinks, or stays the same size.\n\n1 · WORKSPACE SEARCH — sidebar field, ⌘K\nSpans the entire workspace and every media type. This is content search, not a filter over nav item names. On mobile this lived on Home only, behind the Search FAB; on web it is always present because the sidebar is always present.\n\n2 · IN-VIEW SEARCH — field in the page header, /\nScoped to THIS saved view only. Searching inside \"Backlog\" can never return a movie. It narrows what is already on screen. Nothing is added or removed from the library.\n\n3 · ONLINE SEARCH — the Add button, top right\nQueries ONE external provider for things you do not own yet. Its rows say \"Add\", not \"Open\". Always scoped to the view's media type, so the provider list is derived and never asked for.\n\nThe mobile design used an orange + FAB for online search. Web has no FAB — a persistent header button carries the same meaning and keeps the primary action where every other page action already lives."
                },
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "id": "CRfuv",
                  "name": "Note / In-View Search & Filters",
                  "width": 490,
                  "height": 0,
                  "content": "IN-VIEW SEARCH, FILTERS & LAYOUT\n\nThe header search field is always visible on web rather than collapsing out of a magnifier — there is room, and a visible field advertises that the view is searchable. \"/\" focuses it.\n\n· The subtitle slot (\"Books · 10 of 128\") becomes the live result count while searching.\n\n· The FILTERS control is HIDDEN during search, exactly as on mobile. Filters configure the saved view itself; they do not apply to search results. Showing both would imply a composition that does not happen.\n\n· The LAYOUT switcher STAYS visible during search — this is the one place web diverges. Mobile hid it only as a side effect of it living inside the filter sheet. Grid/list/table has no semantic conflict with searching, so there is no reason to hide it.\n\nZero matches is the state that matters. It offers the escape hatch into online search and carries the typed query across, so the user never retypes it.\n\nFILTERS POPOVER\nOn mobile this was a bottom sheet holding LAYOUT, SORT and FILTERS. On web it is a popover anchored to the Filters button and holds SORT and FILTERS only.\n\nLAYOUT was promoted out of it and into the header. It only moved into the sheet on mobile to free the bottom-right corner for the Add FAB. Web has no FAB and has horizontal room, so the reason no longer applies."
                },
                {
                  "type": "note",
                  "id": "vzgQ1",
                  "name": "Note / Online Search & Create",
                  "width": 490,
                  "height": 0,
                  "content": "ONLINE SEARCH & CREATE\n\nReached from the Add button, or from the escape hatch in a failed in-view search. It is a MODAL, not a route — you are topping up the view you are already looking at, and you should land back in it.\n\n· ONE provider at a time, never aggregated. The chosen provider decides which record becomes your library entity and supplies its metadata from then on, so it is a deliberate provenance choice — not a filter. Chips stay pinned above the results; the last choice is remembered per media type.\n\n· Items you already own show \"In library\" instead of \"Add\", so a duplicate cannot be created.\n\nMANUAL CREATE is intentionally gated behind a failed online search — it is only offered from the empty state. Manual entries are the main source of duplicate and junk records, so the online lookup is kept as the path of least resistance.\n\nAn EMPTY SAVED VIEW gets an inline CTA rather than a lone button, and dims its search / layout / filter controls since there is nothing yet to search, arrange or filter. The Add button stays live — it is the only thing worth doing on that screen.\n\nTABLE VIEW gains columns on web (author, rating, date added) that mobile had no room for. The column set is per media type."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
